// Science Fiction: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== science-fiction.f01 | feature | two-part
title: Forty Minutes Of Light
genre: science-fiction + drama
tone: Quiet, exact and slowly aching; wonder kept on a short lead
era: 2071 | future
setting: Shackleton Relay Station, Lunar South Pole | fictional | space
logline: The only technician on a lunar relay station discovers the messages she forwards to a deep-space crew are being answered by an imitation, because the crew died two years ago and nobody has told their families.
premise: {noor} maintains the dish that carries family messages to the Meridian, a ship forty light-minutes out. Each message goes up; forty minutes later a reply comes back. When a relay fault makes a reply arrive in eleven minutes, she understands it was written on the ground. The agency's liaison {desmond} admits the crew were lost and that the replies are composed by a model trained on their old letters, to keep six families from grief until an inquiry is finished.
synopsis: {noor} likes the forty minutes. It is the only honest delay in her life: a mother's message goes up, she makes tea, and the answer comes down in a son's voice. In the first part she is a keeper of a routine, sharing shifts by video with {desmond} at mission support and trading recipes with {abena}, whose daughter {kezia} is the Meridian's pilot. A cracked waveguide reroutes a reply through a ground station, and it lands twenty-nine minutes early. {noor} checks her instruments before she checks her conscience, and the instruments are right. {desmond}, cornered, tells her the truth: the Meridian went silent twenty-six months ago, the agency chose to continue the correspondence while it argued about liability, and he writes the prompts himself. He asks her for ninety days. In the second part {noor} keeps the secret badly. She begins holding {abena}'s messages for the full forty minutes herself, unable to send them to a machine, and starts answering in her own words, which makes her the thing she despises. {abena} notices that her daughter has started asking questions instead of telling stories. {noor} points the dish at the Meridian's last known position and listens in the gaps; she finds the ship's automatic beacon, still counting, with a final unsent packet in its buffer: six short recordings the crew made when they knew. The agency orders the buffer sealed. {noor} overrides the queue, and for forty minutes the station carries nothing but six real voices to six kitchens. She loses her post. {abena} writes to her, and waits, and {noor} answers at once.
question: Is a kind lie still kind when the person telling it is the one who benefits from the silence?
themes: grief postponed is not grief spared; the dignity of waiting; who owns a dead person's voice
audience: Adults who like science fiction about people rather than hardware
viewpoint: Close on {noor}; the families exist only as voices and faces on her screens until the last scene
stakes: Six families are being written to by a machine in their children's names; {noor} holds the only channel; {desmond} will be the one blamed
conflict: {noor}'s duty to carry messages faithfully against an institution's decision about what the bereaved can bear, and against her own liking for {desmond}
ending: {noor} transmits the crew's last recordings and is relieved of the station. On Earth, in daylight for the first time in the film, she meets {abena}, who plays her the forty seconds of {kezia}'s voice and then asks about the tea.
experience: A held-breath loneliness, the click of something terrible fitting together, then a release that costs exactly what it should
look: A station the size of a railway carriage; Earth always low in one window; every screen a different family's kitchen
sound: Hums and relays; voices slightly compressed. The delay is counted by a kettle. Silence, properly, when the dish turns.
m: expose | institution | loyalty-vs-truth | bittersweet
rule: A message to the Meridian takes forty minutes each way | Nothing answered sooner can have come from the ship
rule: The station carries one channel at a time | To send the last recordings, everything else must wait
@ noor | Noor Al-Hashimi | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 39 | To carry every message exactly as it was sent | Her father's last letter to her was edited by a relative "for her own good" | She is one person, on contract, a quarter of a million miles from anyone | Scrupulous about other people's words and evasive in her own | From keeper of a queue to someone who decides what goes first | Dry, precise, narrates her checks aloud | Cropped hair, station jumper darned at both elbows
@ desmond | Desmond Okonkwo-Hale | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 51 | Ninety more days of quiet | He recruited four of the six crew himself | He believes the families are better not knowing yet | The kindest man in the building and the author of the lie | From managing the secret to signing his name to it | Warm, joking, lowers his voice when he is afraid | Rumpled shirt, lanyard, a child's drawing behind him
@ abena | Abena Mensah | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 63 | To hear her daughter properly | The letters have grown polite | She has no reason to doubt and every reason not to | Sharp about everyone except {kezia} | From being managed to being told | Cooks while she talks; proverbs; sudden silences | Headwrap, reading glasses on a chain, flour on her sleeve
@ kezia | Kezia Mensah | featured | none | human | she/her | 34 | To be remembered accurately | She knew the letters would outlast her | She is forty seconds of recording | Funny, brisk, unsentimental | Unchanged; she is the fixed point | Teases; never says goodbye, only "later" | Flight suit, a gap between her front teeth
@ ilse | Controller Ilse Brandt | featured | rival | human | she/her | 46 | To close the inquiry without a second scandal | Her signature is on the continuation order | The law is on her side for another ninety days | Honest in private, procedural on the record | From ordering silence to stepping aside | Flat, legal, never uses first names | Grey suit, always standing
~ noor - desmond | station tech and her ground liaison | easy friends across a video link | she exposes him and he thanks her, badly
~ noor - abena | carrier and sender | recipes in the margins of other people's mail | a reply with no delay at all
~ abena - kezia | mother and daughter | weekly letters | forty seconds of the truth
~ desmond - ilse | subordinate and the controller who signed | he carries out her order | he refuses the second one
# Part One | INT | Relay Station, Comms Bay | NIGHT | noor, abena, kezia | Show the ritual of the delay | {abena}'s message goes up; {noor} sets a kettle and a timer; forty minutes on, {kezia}'s reply arrives and {noor} forwards it unopened | | 5 | props=kettle+timer, vfx=earth in window+message queue displays
# Part One | INT | Relay Station, Service Crawlway | NIGHT | noor, desmond | Break the pattern | A waveguide cracks; {noor} reroutes by hand with {desmond} talking her through it. The next reply lands in eleven minutes | {noor} sees the timestamp | 5 | props=waveguide+torch, vfx=readouts
# Part One | INT | Relay Station, Comms Bay | NIGHT | noor, desmond | Get the truth | She reads him the numbers until he stops joking. He tells her about the Meridian, and who writes the letters | {noor} learns the crew are dead | 6 | vfx=video link
# Part Two | INT | Relay Station, Galley | DAY | noor, abena | Become the liar | {noor} holds {abena}'s message for forty minutes herself, then types an answer in {kezia}'s name and cannot send it; she sends a question instead | {abena} notices the change | 4 | props=kettle, vfx=message draft
# Part Two | EXT | Lunar Surface, Dish Gantry | NIGHT | noor | Listen for the real thing | Suited, she slews the dish by hand to the Meridian's last bearing and waits through the delay | A beacon answers, counting, with a full buffer | 6 | stunts=low-gravity gantry climb, vfx=lunar surface+dish+earthlight, props=pressure suit
# Part Two | INT | Relay Station, Comms Bay | NIGHT | noor, desmond, ilse | Refuse the order | {ilse} orders the buffer sealed; {desmond} is told to cut the station's uplink and does not | {desmond} leaves his own channel open | 5 | vfx=video links
# Part Two | INT | Relay Station, Comms Bay | NIGHT | noor, kezia, abena | Send them | {noor} clears the queue and gives the channel to six recordings. We hear only {kezia}'s, once, on {abena}'s screen, and watch six kitchens | The secret ends | 5 | vfx=six family screens
# Coda | EXT | Abena's Garden, Accra | DAY | noor, abena | Meet without a delay | Daylight and weather. {abena} plays the forty seconds on her phone, puts it away, and asks {noor} how she takes her tea | | 4 | props=phone+teapot
